        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(signal): add guided setup wizard",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where the Signal setup wizard could leave users with a configured-looking channel even when the selected Signal transport was not actually ready. In particular, the Docker/container path needed to create or reuse a loopback-only `signal-cli-rest-api` container, prove the linked account and receive transport are usable, and avoid saving partial config when setup fails.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe Signal plugin now owns a guided Docker setup path, account-scoped `apiMode`, and readiness probing that distinguishes daemon reachability from linked-account and receive-stream readiness. Core setup flow now honors a setup wizard's `setupApplied: false` result so failed setup can roll config back instead of recording account selection or partial credentials.\n\nThe PR also adds a narrow plugin SDK QR terminal subpath so setup plugins can render terminal QR codes without importing broader runtime surfaces.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers can run `openclaw channels add --channel signal`, choose the recommended Docker setup, scan a linked-device QR/link when needed, and then verify the channel with `openclaw channels status --probe`. If Docker or linking fails, setup reports what happened and does not save a misleading Signal config. Existing external Signal REST servers remain supported through the existing-server setup path.\n\n## Evidence\n\nReviewer-checkable behavior:\n\n- Docker setup creates or starts `bbernhard/signal-cli-rest-api:0.100` on `127.0.0.1`, uses `MODE=json-rpc`, persists Signal state in a Docker volume, and saves `apiMode: \"container\"` only after setup succeeds.\n- Signal probes now report `readiness:ready`, `readiness:account-missing`, `readiness:receive-unavailable`, or `readiness:daemon-unreachable`; `channels status --probe` no longer treats an unlinked but reachable container as working.\n- The documented Compose example binds the Signal REST API to loopback with `127.0.0.1:8080:8080`.\n- No screenshots: this changes CLI/TUI setup behavior, channel runtime contracts, plugin metadata, and docs, not browser-visible UI.\n\nAutomated and local checks:\n\n- `codex review --uncommitted`: clean native Phase 1 on the final target.\n- Final cold review subagent: clean Phase 2 on the same final target.\n- `/Users/jmerhi/repos/skills/skills/code-review/scripts/check-review-models`: passed.\n- `pnpm -s build`: passed inside native review.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/signal/src/core.test.ts extensions/signal/src/client-adapter.test.ts src/flows/channel-setup.test.ts src/commands/channels.surfaces-signal-runtime-errors-channels-status-output.test.ts src/plugins/official-external-plugin-catalog.test.ts`: passed in final cold review, 200 tests across 4 shards.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/plugins/official-external-plugin-catalog.test.ts src/plugins/contracts/package-manifest.contract.test.ts`: passed, 92 tests.\n- `node --import tsx scripts/sync-plugin-versions.ts --check`: passed.\n- `pnpm exec oxfmt --check --threads=1 extensions/signal/package.json docs/channels/signal.md`: passed.\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n\nNot run:\n\n- Live Signal messaging and manual Signal setup were intentionally not run; this PR keeps the Signal account safe and does not send Signal messages.\n",
